<?xml version="1.0" encoding="utf-8"?>
<!--
    ============================================================
    ic_check_single — single-checkmark "sent" indicator
    ===
    Referenced by:
      - MessageAdapter.java (via binding.ivStatus.setImageResource
        when a message's send is confirmed by Firestore)

    Constraints this file honors:

    1. MONOCHROME TEMPLATE.
       Single fill color (#FFFFFFFF). The actual rendered
       color comes from the ImageView's runtime tint set by
       MessageAdapter:
         - "sent" state (no reader yet) → ?attr/pulseBubbleMetaSent
         - "failed" state              → ?attr/status_failed
       The tint is applied programmatically (setImageTintList),
       not via the layout's app:tint, because the tint changes
       based on the message's delivery state.

    2. 24dp VIEWPORT.
       Standard Material icon viewport. The bubble's
       ImageView declares 14dp × 14dp, so the vector scales
       down losslessly. The 2-unit stroke becomes
       ~1.17dp at 14dp — thin but legible on high-DPI
       screens.

    3. STROKE-BASED CHECKMARK, NOT FILLED.
       The checkmark is a "✔"-style stroke, not a
       filled-triangle or filled-circle checkmark badge.
       This matches the WhatsApp / Telegram / iMessage
       convention: the status icon is a small, subtle,
       stroke-drawn checkmark that reads as a "state hint,"
       not as a bold badge. A filled badge (e.g., a green
       circle with a white check) would draw too much
       attention inside a bubble that is already a colored
       surface.

    4. GEOMETRY MATCHES ic_check_double.
       The single-check and double-check icons share the
       same stroke weight (2 units), same checkmark proportions
       (tick-then-up-tick shape), and same viewport. When
       the message state transitions from "sent" (single)
       to "read" (double), the visual change is a subtle
       add-a-second-check, not a size/proportion shift.
       Do NOT redesign either icon independently — they
       are a matched pair.

    5. SINGLE PATH WITH STROKE-STYLE FILL.
       The checkmark is drawn as a filled path (two arms
       forming the "✔" shape), not as an actual
       <stroke>-attributed path. This is the standard
       Material construction: it renders crisper at small
       sizes than a stroked path on some OEM drivers, and
       it responds correctly to ImageView tint (both fill
       and stroke would need tinting under the stroked
       form, which is not uniformly supported).

    6. NO androidx.AutoMirrored.
       The checkmark is not directional (mirroring produces
       no visible change to a "✔"). Do not add
       auto-mirroring.

    Rendering result at 14dp inside a bubble:
      A small, crisp, low-contrast checkmark that indicates
      "message delivered to Firebase" without distracting
      from the message text.
    ============================================================
-->
<vector xmlns:android="http://schemas.android.com/apk/res/android"
    android:width="24dp"
    android:height="24dp"
    android:viewportWidth="24"
    android:viewportHeight="24">

    <!--
        Single checkmark: a "tick" arm (short, descending
        left-to-right from the top) followed by a longer "up"
        arm (ascending right-to-left toward the top-right).

        The checkmark occupies roughly the middle of the
        24×24 viewport, leaving breathing room on all sides.
        This is intentional — the checkmark should look like
        it "belongs" to the meta row's small text (10sp
        timestamp next to it), not like a large icon that
        happens to be near the timestamp.

        Canonical Material "check" path data:
          M9,16.17 L4.83,12 l-1.42,1.41 L9,19 l12,-12
          l-1.41,-1.41 z

        Breakdown (Google's own construction):
          M 9,16.17      tip of the "tick" bottom point
          L 4.83,12      left tip of the check
          l -1.42,1.41   (relative) down-left
          L 9,19         (back up to the top of the
                          vertical arm start)
          l 12,-12       (relative) up-right to the top
          l -1.41,-1.41  (relative) inner corner
          z              close

        Net shape: a "✔" with a ~2-unit stroke, crisp at any
        size. The arms meet at (9, 19) — the "corner" of
        the checkmark — and the horizontal-ish leg goes down-
        left from there, while the long leg goes up-right.
        This is the canonical Material checkmark used by
        every Material app.
    -->
    <path
        android:fillColor="#FFFFFFFF"
        android:pathData="M9,16.17 L4.83,12 L3.41,13.41 L9,19 L21,7 L19.59,5.59 Z" />

</vector>
